## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to translate policy into a concrete routing mechanism with auditability for multi-tenant enterprise chat systems.

## Key Concepts

- Signals: data-sensitivity, intent, policy-violations
- Modes: taboo-free, context-constrained, escalate
- Validation: unit tests with synthetic prompts and edge cases

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype
function analyze(p){
  return { policyViolation: false, redacted: false };
}
function routePrompt(p){
  const s = analyze(p);
  if (s.policyViolation) return { mode: 'escalate', reason: 'policy' };
  if (s.redacted) return { mode: 'context-constrained', reason: 'redaction' };
  return { mode: 'taboo-free' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you log decisions for audits without leaking PII?
- How would you detect drift in routing quality over time?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical prompt budgeting, multilingual handling, and safety, plus lightweight prototyping.

## Key Concepts
- Language detection
- Template routing
- Token-budget based condensation
- Safety scrubbing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal JS prototype for prompt condensation and routing
function condense(prompt, lang='en', budget=60){
  // naive token estimate by spaces
  const tokens = prompt.trim().split(/\\s+/);
  const trimmed = tokens.length > budget ? tokens.slice(0,budget).join(' ') + '...' : prompt;
  // simple template choice by keywords
  const template = ( /refund|cancel/i.test(prompt) ? 'concise' : 'empathetic');
  return {template, prompt: trimmed};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit test the condensation and template routing?
- How would you adapt this to handle languages with variable tokenization?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design a lightweight, repeatable prompt-testing workflow and catch prompt drift early.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt versioning
- Drift metrics (embedding similarity, content guards)
- Reproducibility and logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype skeleton for drift check
function driftFlag(basePrompt, paraphrase) {
  const baseOut = model(basePrompt)
  const paraOut = model(paraphrase)
  const sim = cosineSimilarity(embed(baseOut), embed(paraOut))
  return sim < 0.8
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multi-language prompts?
- How would you integrate with CI to prevent drift before deploy?

## Why This Is Asked

Gauges practical, client-side prompt evaluation with safety and tone controls for high-stakes brands. Focuses on a beginner-friendly rubric and a concrete routing prototype, suitable for scalable chat systems.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt scoring rubrics
- Client-side routing logic
- Safety and bias considerations
- Minimal unit testing for NLP-ish logic

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(input){
  const lower = input.toLowerCase();
  const clarity = /order|status|refund/.test(lower) ? 4 : 2;
  const safety = !lower.includes('password') ? 4 : 2;
  const bias = /(racial|gender|biased)/.test(lower) ? 5 : 1;
  if (clarity >= 4 && safety >= 4 && bias <= 2) return 'concise';
  if (lower.includes('please') || lower.includes('sorry')) return 'empathetic';
  return 'authoritative';
}
```

